#!/bin/sh
#
# QDM530: seed the cellular WAN. The WAN is the Quectel RG520N-EB 5G modem on
# PCIe/MHI, driven by wwand. The modem exposes no cdc-wdm control node - its QMI
# runs on the QRTR bus (mhi0_IPCR via qcom_mhi_qrtr) - so wwand's QMI-over-QRTR
# transport is selected with the sentinel `option device 'qrtr'`. The data path
# is rmnet/QMAP on the MHI net device mhi_hwip0 (mux child wwand0, mux_id 1).
#
# wwand is configured network-native (everything in /etc/config/network): a
# wwand_modem section for the hardware and a proto-wwand interface for the
# connection. ucidef in board.d cannot express a proto-wwand interface, so it is
# seeded here instead.
#
# The WDA data-format endpoint (ep_type PCIE / ep_id 4) and the QMAP version are
# auto-derived by wwand (>= 1.7.0_pre7): the PCIe/MHI endpoint comes from the data
# netdev's resolved sysfs path and the QMAP ladder negotiates the version itself
# (lands on v5 here), so no overrides are seeded - HW-verified on this board. If you
# run an older wwand that needs them, add network.wwmodem.ep_type='3', ep_id='4',
# qmap_version='1' back. pdp_type is ipv4v6 (dual-stack works); the modem continues
# IPv4-only if the carrier rejects the IPv6 PDP. APN is left to the SIM/network
# default - set network.wwmodem.apn (or network.wan.apn) if the carrier needs one.
#
# Recovery ladder: wwand climbs it on repeated activation failures, firing a
# hardware rung at the attempt counts in `recovery_steps` and finally a board
# reboot at `failreboot` + 1 (upstream: 8/16/24, failreboot 100).
#
# This board carried a COMPRESSED ladder (4/10/14, failreboot 15) on the
# reasoning that no hardware rung could do anything here, so the reboot was the
# only one that worked. HW 2026-11-01 retired both halves of that reasoning and
# showed the compression doing real harm, so it is gone.
#
# What the rungs actually do here:
#   - `opmode_cycle` sets the radio to low power and brings it back. If the
#     bring-back fails the radio is LEFT off, in the modem, surviving reboots
#     and a mains power cycle. Observed: `SET_OPERATING_MODE(online)` answered
#     DEVICE_NOT_READY (QMI 52) for 126 attempts afterwards.
#   - `modem_reset` is worse: twice, a reset left the modem gone from PCIe
#     entirely (all MHI channels, link down) and a board reboot did NOT bring it
#     back. Only a modem power cycle did.
#   - `usb_repower` is still a no-op, but NOT because the board has no modem
#     power line - it has one, and the earlier claim that it did not was wrong.
#     The vendor drives GPIO 29/27/28 from userspace with hard-coded numbers
#     (no device-tree node, which is why the DTB search missed them). What that
#     sequence does is only half known: replaying it from the shell turned the
#     modem OFF and did NOT bring it back (GPIO23 1->0 and stayed; a board
#     reboot is what restored it). The vendor's gaps are 3 ms and 70 ms and
#     busybox `sleep` here cannot express them (FLOAT_DURATION off => whole
#     seconds), so a correctly-timed replay has never been tried and the ON
#     half is unproven. Earlier notes credited this sequence with recovering
#     the modem twice; that reading did not survive the replay.
#     The rung no-ops only because wwand has no board profile naming those GPIOs
#     (see the userspace-GPIO section of docs/qdm530/aer-hang.md). Wiring them
#     in is the open work - but it would only complete the recovery ladder if
#     the ON half turns out to work, which is exactly what is not known.
#
# And the failure that drove the compressed ladder all the way to a reboot was
# not a board fault at all: a SIM with no service. Registration that never
# completes is NORMAL for a CPE - a lapsed SIM, a carrier outage, bad coverage -
# and escalating that to a wedged modem and a reboot is strictly worse than
# waiting. So: upstream's first threshold, and the two rungs that have been
# observed to make things worse are pushed out of reach until the GPIO repower
# exists to pair with them. failreboot 0 (never) because a reboot has never once
# recovered this modem - only a power cycle of the modem itself has.
#
# zero_rx_timeout 0 (OFF; upstream default 21600 = 6 h). It was 120 s here, and
# that was a mistake born of a wrong model of what the watchdog does.
#
# The watchdog itself: wwand's rx_stall_watch trips when the cumulative rx
# counter has stood still for this long. It reads only rx -- TX is never
# consulted -- so on a CGNAT'd cellular link with an idle LAN it cannot tell a
# dead bearer from a quiet one. /usr/sbin/qdm530-wan-keepalive keeps one echo
# per 30 s flowing to make "no rx" mean dead rather than idle, but that only
# works while the probe is answered: if it goes unanswered for a reason upstream
# of the modem -- ICMP filtered, a carrier outage, a routing problem -- then rx
# stands still on a link whose bearer is fine.
#
# WHAT HAPPENS ON A TRIP IS NOT THE LADDER. An earlier version of this comment
# said a false positive would "climb the ladder to a reboot", and the ladder
# values above were raised partly to contain that. They do not contain it at
# all: modem_common.trip_zero_rx() consults recovery_steps nowhere. It asks
# rec.can_repower(), and if that is false it calls self.reset() directly --
# wwand's own docs say as much ("on a board that can repower it repowers, else
# it falls through to a modem reset"). can_repower() is
# `opts.can_repower ? opts.can_repower() : (opts.repower != null)`, and this
# board ships no wwand board profile, so it is false and the reset is what runs.
# failreboot=0 does not gate it either; that only disables the final reboot rung.
#
# And a modem reset is the one action on this board with an observed
# catastrophic outcome: twice it left the modem gone from PCIe entirely, with
# every MHI channel down, recovered only by cutting mains power. So the
# watchdog's only available remedy here is worse than the fault it detects, and
# a detector that can false-positive into that is a liability however long its
# timeout is -- 6 h would make it rarer, not safe.
#
# Off until there is a safe remedy to offer it. A candidate remedy exists but is
# NOT yet one: the vendor drives GPIO 29/27/28 as a modem power sequence (see
# the userspace-GPIO section of docs/qdm530/aer-hang.md). Only its OFF half is
# demonstrated here - a shell replay powered the modem down and a board reboot,
# not the sequence, brought it back - and the vendor's 3 ms / 70 ms gaps cannot
# be reproduced with busybox `sleep` (whole seconds only). Wiring these into a
# wwand board profile would make can_repower() true, so doing that BEFORE the
# ON half is proven would re-arm this detector onto a remedy that is only known
# to turn the modem off. Prove the powered-ON replay first.
#
# What is lost meanwhile: a bearer that is up but dead is no longer
# auto-recovered. The connection-attempt ladder still covers a bearer that
# fails to CONNECT (opmode_cycle at attempt 8). wwand's native answer for the
# rest is `context_failed` on the ubus interface -- something that actually
# understands routes and reachability decides, and the daemon acts -- which is
# the arrangement to move to rather than re-shortening this timeout.
#
# wwand's own answer to this is NOT an in-daemon probe: ddimension/wwand#13 was
# closed having rejected one ("An ICMP probe is squarely L3: it needs a source
# address, a route, a firewall zone, and DNS if the target is a name"), and the
# maintainer implemented `context_failed` on the ubus interface instead
# (src-ucode/ubus.uc, daemon.uc, with a rate limit and a unit test). The
# intended arrangement is that something which actually understands routes and
# reachability decides, calls context_failed, and lets the daemon run the rungs
# it cannot reach itself -- while zero_rx_timeout stays at its conservative
# default as a backstop rather than as the detector.
#
# Moving to that is a policy change for this board, not a bug fix: it trades a
# detector that can false-positive into a reboot for one that depends on an
# external monitor being correct and running. Left as it is deliberately, with
# the trade-off written down.
. /lib/functions.sh

[ "$(board_name)" = "unbranded,qdm530" ] || exit 0

# idempotent: only seed when the modem section is absent, so a config-preserving
# sysupgrade (or a user who edited the WAN) is never overwritten.
if ! uci -q get network.wwmodem >/dev/null; then
	uci -q batch <<-EOF
		set network.wwmodem='wwand_modem'
		set network.wwmodem.device='qrtr'
		set network.wwmodem.protocol='qmi'
		set network.wwmodem.netdev='mhi_hwip0'
		set network.wwmodem.recovery_steps='8 100000 200000'
		set network.wwmodem.failreboot='0'
		set network.wwmodem.zero_rx_timeout='0'
	EOF
fi

if ! uci -q get network.wan >/dev/null; then
	uci -q batch <<-EOF
		set network.wan='interface'
		set network.wan.proto='wwand'
		set network.wan.device='wwand0'
		set network.wan.modem='wwmodem'
		set network.wan.autosetup='1'
		set network.wan.mux_id='1'
		set network.wan.pdp_type='ipv4v6'
	EOF
fi

uci -q commit network

exit 0
